The quadratic pion scalar radius, $\langle r^2 \rangle_S^\pi$, is a fundamental parameter of low-energy Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) and plays a key role in testing Chiral Perturbation Theory predictions. A long-standing tension exists between the high determination by Yndur\'ain ($0.75 \pm 0.07 \text{ fm}^2$) and the lower values derived from standard Muskhelishvili--Omn\`es dispersive treatments ($0.61 \pm 0.04 \text{ fm}^2$). In this paper, we re-examine the dispersive representation of the pion scalar form factor. We show that strictly enforcing phase-shift continuity across the intermediate-energy elasticity threshold requires the scalar form factor to possess a zero in the complex plane. Incorporating this zero into the Muskhelishvili--Omn\`es integral equation modifies the underlying Omn\`es function and naturally reconciles the two disparate formalisms. We present an updated and consistent determination of the quadratic pion scalar radius to be $\langle r^2 \rangle_S^\pi = 0.65 \pm 0.05 \text{ fm}^2$, effectively resolving the historical discrepancy and providing a accurate baseline for hadronic precision observables.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Pion scalar radius, Dispersion relations, Muskhelishvili--Omnès equation, Chiral Perturbation Theory, Phase shifts.

